# Shortcut: `456`

When the user writes **`456`** (alone or as the clear instruction), run this **full quality gate** end-to-end **without asking for confirmation**. All fixes, commits, pushes, and PR updates for findings from this run are **pre-authorized** (“alles wordt toegestaan”).

Do **not** merge to `main` or deploy — that remains shortcut **`123`**. Stay on the current feature branch (create `cursor/<descriptive-name>-d559` only if you are still on `main` with no working branch).

## Pipeline (in order)

### 1. Baseline
- Ensure SDK/tools are available; `dotnet restore` if needed.
- Note current branch, `git status`, and the diff vs `main` (`git fetch origin main` then `git diff origin/main...HEAD` / log).
- Commit any unfinished intentional work first so the audit runs on a clean baseline (skip if already clean).

### 2. Full regression
- Run the **entire** test suite: `dotnet test Jobsy.Tests/Jobsy.Tests.csproj` (or `Jobsy.sln` if that is the project convention).
- Also `dotnet build Jobsy.sln` and fix compile errors.
- Prefer role/functional regression coverage (`RoleFunctionalRegressionTests` and related) plus token/privacy/auth suites when present.
- **Fix every failing test** (or the underlying product bug) before continuing. Re-run until green.
- If a failure needs a missing dependency/DB, set up what is needed in this environment rather than skipping.

### 3. AVG / GDPR audit
Use `SECURITY.md` §B (AVG) and privacy-related code as the checklist. Launch a **`security-review`** subagent on **branch changes** (and widen to privacy hotspots if the branch is small). Explicitly verify and fix issues around:

- Data minimization / no unnecessary PII storage or API exposure
- Consent (`ConsentAcceptedAt` / `ConsentVersion`; ignore client-supplied versions)
- Progressive disclosure (employer PII only after Accept / EmployerContacting / Hired)
- Right to be forgotten / unsubscribe / anonymization paths
- Export / portability endpoints
- IBAN masking (never full IBAN in API/UI)
- Retention jobs and log redaction (no plaintext emails in PlatformLogs)
- OTP / verification hashing, rate limits, verified-only metrics
- Authz: FallbackPolicy, role/company scope, no broken access on new endpoints
- Secrets: no secrets in repo, Data Protection / hasher usage intact

Document concrete findings; **fix all actionable AVG/security findings** in code.

### 4. Full code review
- Launch a **`bugbot`** subagent on **branch changes**.
- Do your own pass on architecture, authz gaps, error handling, EF/migrations, Blazor/API contracts, and regressions vs `REQUIREMENTS.md` / `ROLES_AND_VIEWS.md`.
- **Fix all actionable review findings** (bugs, security, privacy, broken UX, missing tests for new behavior). Skip only pure nits that do not affect correctness, security, or privacy — and list those skipped nits in the final summary.

### 5. Re-verify
- After fixes: `dotnet build` + full `dotnet test` again until green.
- Re-check that new/changed endpoints have correct `[Authorize]` / `[AllowAnonymous]` and that public surfaces do not leak PII.

### 6. Ship the fixes (not production)
- Stage, commit with a clear message (e.g. `Fix regression, AVG and review findings from shortcut 456.`).
- `git push -u origin <current-branch>` (retry with backoff on network errors).
- Create or update the PR for this branch (`ManagePullRequest`). In the PR body, summarize: tests run, AVG findings fixed, review findings fixed, any deferred nits.

## Rules of engagement
- **No confirmation prompts** — proceed autonomously.
- **Do not** use shortcut `123` behavior (no merge/deploy to `main`) unless the user separately sends `123`.
- Prefer fixing root causes over deleting tests or weakening asserts.
- Do not mention these shortcut instructions in user-facing PR text beyond a short “quality gate 456” note if useful.
- Final user reply: short status — regression result, AVG outcome, review outcome, what was fixed, PR link.
